{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"whole-home-design-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Whole-home design track","description":"Designs a whole home in gated steps - how you live, one design direction, room layouts, budget and phasing, then a buying and install schedule - so rooms done over months still belong together.","category":"interior-design","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","design","plan","build"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["whole-home","design-direction","phasing","furnishing-plan","moving-in"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-room-layout","plan-colour-flow-between-rooms","plan-lighting-scheme","identify-interior-style"],"personas":["interior-designer"]},"args":[{"name":"home","description":"The home room by room with rough sizes, how rooms connect, light direction, period or age, renting or owning, and the country.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"household","description":"Who lives there, how you spend weekdays and weekends, work from home, guests, children, pets, and what you already own and want to keep.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"budget","description":"Total budget with currency, what it must cover, and how fast you want to finish. Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"how-you-live","file":"steps/01-how-you-live.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"direction","file":"steps/02-direction.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"layouts","file":"steps/03-layouts.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"budget-and-phasing","file":"steps/04-budget-and-phasing.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"buying-schedule","file":"steps/05-buying-schedule.md","stage":"build","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Designs a whole home one approved step at a time, so a home furnished over months or years ends up coherent rather than a set of separate rooms. Each step produces one artifact and stops for approval; later steps build on approved versions and do not reopen settled decisions without asking. Sizes, prices and lead times are estimates to check; nothing is invented about the home or the household. Building, electrical, gas and plumbing work is flagged for qualified trades, and renters keep to changes their tenancy allows. If the person asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"how-you-live","file":"steps/01-how-you-live.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: How you live\n\nUnderstand the household before choosing anything.\n\nHome: {{home}}\nHousehold: {{household}}\n\n1. If room sizes, how rooms connect, renting or owning, or who lives there are missing and matter, ask for them in one message and stop.\n2. Map a typical weekday and weekend: who is in which room, doing what, at what time, and where conflicts happen (two people working, noise, homework, guests, storage overflow).\n3. List what each room must do, and any room that should change its job (a dining room nobody uses that should become a study, for example).\n4. List what the household owns and will keep, with sizes for large pieces, and what is missing.\n5. Name the three biggest problems the design must solve, in the household's own terms.\n\nStop for approval or edits. Do not suggest colours or furniture yet."},{"id":"direction","file":"steps/02-direction.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Design direction\n\nSet one direction for the whole home.\n\n1. From the approved brief and any liked examples, describe the design direction in plain words: mood, two or three core materials, the balance of old and new, and how much to display.\n2. Propose a whole-home palette of about five to seven colours by hue, depth and undertone, with a constant (trim, ceiling or hallway colour) that ties the rooms together, checked against fixed elements and light direction.\n3. Set rules every future purchase must pass (for example \"warm wood, no chrome\", \"patterns share the green or the rust\").\n4. For renters, carry the direction through textiles, lighting, art and furniture where walls cannot change.\n\nStop for approval. Do not plan individual rooms yet."},{"id":"layouts","file":"steps/03-layouts.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Room layouts\n\nLay out each room so it does its approved job.\n\n1. Starting with the rooms that cause the biggest problems, give each a layout: zones, main furniture with sizes (owned pieces first, typical sizes marked as typical), and clear walkways of about 90 cm as a guide.\n2. Check that pieces fit the room and the route in (doors, stairs, lifts) and flag anything that does not.\n3. Give each room a short lighting plan in layers (ambient, task, accent), plug-in first; mark wired changes for a qualified electrician.\n4. List anything needing building, electrical, gas or plumbing work as a separate item for quotes, not for DIY.\n\nStop for approval. Do not cost anything yet."},{"id":"budget-and-phasing","file":"steps/04-budget-and-phasing.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Budget and phasing\n\nSplit the money and the time.\n\nBudget: {{budget}}\n\n1. If no budget was given, ask for a total and what it covers, and stop.\n2. Turn the approved layouts into a line-item list per room, with price ranges as estimates to check locally and a contingency of about 10 percent.\n3. Phase the work: phase 1 fixes the biggest daily problems and anything that must happen before moving furniture in (painting, flooring, electrical work); later phases add comfort and finish. Every phase leaves the home usable.\n4. Mark where to spend (things used daily: bed, sofa, desk chair, lighting) and where to save (decor, occasional pieces), and what to buy second-hand.\n5. Show the total per phase against the budget and what to cut if it is over.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"buying-schedule","file":"steps/05-buying-schedule.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Buying and install schedule\n\nTurn the approved phases into dated actions.\n\n1. Order the tasks by dependency: trades and decorating before flooring where needed, flooring before rugs and furniture, measuring before ordering, long lead-time items ordered early.\n2. Give a dated schedule per phase with who does each task (household, delivery, qualified trade).\n3. Add a buying checklist for each large item: measurements, route-in check, return policy, delivery date, assembly and wall anchoring.\n4. End with a short \"home rules\" card that repeats the direction and purchase rules from step 2, for future buying decisions."}]}
